            "text": "At the edge of a small town, there was a library that stayed open after midnight. Its windows glowed amber above the empty street, and anyone who passed beneath them could hear the soft weather of pages turning.\n\nMara first entered on a sleepless Tuesday, carrying an umbrella with three broken ribs and a question she could not phrase. The librarian did not ask for a card. He merely pointed toward a staircase labeled, \u201cBooks for Questions That Are Not Ready Yet.\u201d\n\nUpstairs shelves curved like the inside of a shell, holding volumes with titles such as How to Miss a Train Without Losing a Day and What the Moon Keeps in Its Pockets. Mara chose a thin blue book called The Sound of Almost.\n\nIt contained no answers. Instead, each page described a nearly: a cake almost burned, an apology almost spoken, a door almost opened before the storm. As Mara read, her question loosened into smaller questions, and those became things she could hold.\n\nAt dawn, she returned the book to its shelf and found a note tucked behind it: \u201cNot every uncertainty is a locked room. Some are windows.\u201d\n\nOutside, rain had stopped. The town was waking: bakery lights, bicycle bells, a dog arguing with a sparrow. Mara lifted her broken umbrella anyway. It did not keep her dry, but it made a small bright roof around her, and for the first time in weeks, she walked slowly enough to notice the morning arriving everywhere at once."
